<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Every need's rate lives in its needClass, never in this file. NeedDef.fallPerDay exists for parity and is
    read by nothing in this port (RimWorld gives it to Need_Chemical alone) - setting it here is a load error,
    on purpose: it used to be the field a need was silently assumed to decay by, and the assumption was wrong
    for all eight of them. See NeedDef.fallPerDay and Need's own class remarks.
  -->

  <NeedDef>
    <defName>Food</defName>
    <label>food</label>
    <description>Nutrition. Falls while awake and asleep; hunger slows as it empties.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Food</needClass>
    <listPriority>100</listPriority>
    <major>true</major>
    <minIntelligence>Animal</minIntelligence>
    <threshPercents>
      <li>0.3</li>
      <li>0.15</li>
    </threshPercents>
  </NeedDef>

  <NeedDef>
    <defName>Rest</defName>
    <label>rest</label>
    <description>Sleep. Falls while awake, rises in bed.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Rest</needClass>
    <listPriority>95</listPriority>
    <major>true</major>
    <minIntelligence>Animal</minIntelligence>
    <threshPercents>
      <li>0.28</li>
      <li>0.14</li>
    </threshPercents>
  </NeedDef>

  <NeedDef>
    <defName>Joy</defName>
    <label>recreation</label>
    <description>Recreation. Falls steadily; restored by varied activities, each with its own tolerance.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Joy</needClass>
    <listPriority>90</listPriority>
    <major>true</major>
    <minIntelligence>Humanlike</minIntelligence>
    <freezeWhileSleeping>true</freezeWhileSleeping>
    <!--
      A prisoner has no recreation. Not a mood choice: this port gives a prisoner no way to obtain joy at
      all - every joy activity is reached through a job, and a prisoner takes none - so the need could only
      ever fall, and a need that can only fall is a permanent mood penalty wearing a need's clothes. RimWorld
      ships the same flag on the same need. Pawn_NeedsTracker.ShouldHaveNeed reads it, and capture, recruit
      and release each re-run that sweep, so the need goes and comes back with the prisoner's status.
    -->
    <neverOnPrisoner>true</neverOnPrisoner>
    <threshPercents>
      <li>0.15</li>
      <li>0.3</li>
      <li>0.7</li>
      <li>0.85</li>
    </threshPercents>
  </NeedDef>

  <NeedDef>
    <defName>Mood</defName>
    <label>mood</label>
    <description>Overall mood: 50% plus the sum of all active thoughts. Drives mental breaks.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Mood</needClass>
    <listPriority>1000</listPriority>
    <major>true</major>
    <minIntelligence>Humanlike</minIntelligence>
    <seekerRisePerHour>0.05</seekerRisePerHour>
    <seekerFallPerHour>0.06</seekerFallPerHour>
    <threshPercents>
      <li>0.35</li>
      <li>0.2</li>
      <li>0.05</li>
    </threshPercents>
  </NeedDef>

  <NeedDef Name="EnvironmentNeedBase" Abstract="True">
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Environment</needClass>
    <minIntelligence>Humanlike</minIntelligence>
    <baseLevel>0.5</baseLevel>
    <seekerRisePerHour>0.3</seekerRisePerHour>
    <seekerFallPerHour>0.3</seekerFallPerHour>
    <freezeWhileSleeping>true</freezeWhileSleeping>
    <showOnNeedList>true</showOnNeedList>
  </NeedDef>

  <!--
    Beauty is the one environment need with a driver (system: needs.beauty): MapEnvironmentSampler reads the
    cells around the citizen through BeautyUtility, so it overrides the base's generic Need_Environment with
    Need_Beauty, which adds the category readout the Beauty ThoughtDef's worker bands mood from.
  -->
  <NeedDef ParentName="EnvironmentNeedBase">
    <defName>Beauty</defName>
    <label>beauty</label>
    <description>How pleasant the surroundings look.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Beauty</needClass>
    <listPriority>80</listPriority>
  </NeedDef>

  <!--
    Comfort and RoomSize are the two needs in this port that genuinely never move, and they say so here
    rather than leaving a reader to infer it from an unset field. Both are seekers whose target comes from
    MapEnvironmentSampler; it answers null for both, null means "the Def's baseLevel", and each starts at
    that same baseLevel - so the target is always exactly where the need already is. The rise/fall rates
    below are real and would apply the instant a target moved; nothing moves one.

    What each is waiting for, from RimWorld (see Need_Environment.CurInstantLevel for the whole shape):
      Comfort  - Need_Comfort is not sampled from surroundings at all. Furniture pushes it: sitting on or
                 lying in a thing calls ComfortUsed(comfort) and the need chases that for 15 ticks before
                 falling back to 0. It needs a comfort hook in the job system and a Comfort stat on
                 furniture, not a map reading.
      RoomSize - Need_RoomSize samples the citizen's room out to a 7.9-cell radius and runs the cell count
                 through a curve. Rooms exist here (Building.RoomTracker), so this one is a branch in
                 MapEnvironmentSampler plus the measurement that branch costs - the same radial sample
                 Beauty already pays 3.5us for outdoors (see that class).
  -->
  <NeedDef ParentName="EnvironmentNeedBase">
    <defName>Comfort</defName>
    <label>comfort</label>
    <description>Comfort from furniture used recently. Holds at its starting level until something pushes comfort at it.</description>
    <listPriority>75</listPriority>
    <seekerRisePerHour>0.5</seekerRisePerHour>
    <seekerFallPerHour>0.2</seekerFallPerHour>
  </NeedDef>

  <!--
    Outdoors is not a seeker and has no sampler: RimWorld's Need_Outdoors reads the roof over the citizen's
    head and the openness of the cell they stand in, and moves itself. It was an EnvironmentNeedBase child
    here until the needs audit, which is why it sat at 0.5 in every game ever played - the sampler answered
    null for it, null means "use baseLevel", and a seeker already on its target never moves.

    It sits here, between two needs it has nothing to do with, only to keep the load order of this file
    unchanged: a NeedDef's index is its position in the loaded database, and the Statistical tier seeds a
    citizen's cohort sample from it (Pawn_TierTracker.ApplyCohortSample). Moving a def would resample every
    untracked citizen in every existing save.
  -->
  <NeedDef>
    <defName>Outdoors</defName>
    <label>outdoors</label>
    <description>Time spent under open sky. Refills quickly outdoors and drains under a roof; a citizen kept inside too long gets cabin fever. Starts full (Need_Outdoors.SetInitialLevel), not at baseLevel.</description>
    <needClass>SimWorld.Needs.Need_Outdoors</needClass>
    <listPriority>70</listPriority>
    <minIntelligence>Humanlike</minIntelligence>
    <threshPercents>
      <li>0.8</li>
      <li>0.6</li>
      <li>0.4</li>
      <li>0.2</li>
      <li>0.05</li>
    </threshPercents>
  </NeedDef>

  <NeedDef ParentName="EnvironmentNeedBase">
    <defName>RoomSize</defName>
    <label>space</label>
    <description>How spacious the current room feels. Holds at its starting level until a room sampler answers for it.</description>
    <listPriority>65</listPriority>
  </NeedDef>

</Defs>
